A||||3||ferg||need advise on koni shocks||||1||
Z||000000||ferg||05-01-2001||01:18 PM||||I'm getting new tires soon and I need to get an alignment as well. Since I've never replaced the shocks, I figure now is a good time to do this.<P>I want to use the koni's and have some questions about them.<P>1) are these red or the yellow?<BR>2) are they adjustable like the pictures firm, med, soft?<BR>3) are they worth the money in a stock set-up? (I'm planning on keeping it stock)<BR>4) What other brands are available/ what do you like?<BR>5) who has the best price? I saw in another post shox.com- good/bad experiences with them<BR>6) any other comments?<P>The car has 50,000 miles, and to my knowledge nothing has ever been done to it. So I'm also, trying to make it road worthy for this summer. Should I replace anything else while I have it apart?<P>I'm open for other ideas, except deviating from stock- (coil-overs, lowering, rear sway bar, etc)<P>Do you think I'll need to perform other maintenance on it?<P>Thanks in advance<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Wannabe||05-01-2001||05:19 PM||||Konis are a very "high end" choice in shocks. They will improve handling and are an excellent choice no doubt, but if you are sticking with stock suspension, it really may not be worth the expense. KYB GR-2 will tighten up the suspension over stock and will be much less expensive.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Slammed Fiero||05-01-2001||05:30 PM||||We have Koni's on My fiero , Corvette , 94-911RSR, 71 911 S, and both Corvairs.. There very well made , and rebuildable , but it comes at a cost. <P>There is no point in Koni's up front , unless you have them in rear. The Problem with the Fiero's suspension isn't total lack of adjustability in the front , it's the soft rear set up. Just get a set of good quality shocks for the front. I know of a few autocrossers that run non adjustable Gm performer and KYB's up front with great success. Save your money. Unless you plan to build the cars suspension up , and if you do plan to take that road , don't start with the front of the car<P><BR>JM||||reg||1
Z||000003||stsmithgt||05-02-2001||09:51 AM||||I thought about Koni's and KYB's, but went with Monroe, because I don't need that type of performance out of a shock with my stock setup.  It wasn't worth the expense.  I just had the shocks and struts put in yesterday and it cost me $443.00.  It would have been cheaper to do it myself, but time, tools, and a place to do it wasn't available.  It also would have been cheaper if I drove somewhere away from Monterey, but the guy that did the work is a friend of my brothers that has a shop so I gave him some business. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I was surprized at the difference in handling.  I would have changed them out years ago if I had known.<P>------------------<BR>Will I ever learn? Ow! that's hot....Ow! that's hot....Ow! that's hot...:0)<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpicsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
